  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1) \nA weak inverted sfc trough will slowly eke wwd overnight, at the same\ntime an upper low approaches the region from the nw. Best dynamics \nfrom the upper low look to be south of the forecast area as the low\nmoves toward the IN/OH/KY region in the afternoon. \n\nShowers are expected to develop in central OH and move west. Cannot \nrule out a bit of thunder given the deep nature of the upper low. \nGreater coverage of showers and rainfall amounts will be from central\nOhio into northeast Kentucky and tail off the further west you go. \n\nAs the mid level low continues off to the southeast to then get\nabsorbed into a larger cut off along the Atlantic coast at the end of\nthe week, surface high pressure centered over Quebec will be able to\nspread back south into the region. This will bring an extended period\nof dry conditions.\n\nHigh temperatures will be below normal through the week and into the\nweekend. With increased low level moisture and a stubborn cloud deck,\nthe diurnal range will be narrowed so that low will be a bit above\nnormal tonight and Wednesday night. Low levels will finally dry out\non Thursday and the cloud deck will erode. So lows will get a bit\nchilly for the latter part of the week.",
